# Collapse pass-throughs after refactor

When a refactor leaves a no-op wrapper or rename, remove it in the same change set.

## Pass-through functions

If a function’s body only calls another function (same arguments, same return), **delete the wrapper** and update callers to call the underlying function.

```js
// ❌ BAD — leftover after fold logic was removed
export function lettersOnly(text) {
  return stripNonLetters(text);
}

// ✅ GOOD — callers use stripNonLetters directly
```

Keep a thin function only when it still adds behavior: composition of multiple steps, a meaningful bound default, or a stable public API that must not rename yet (document why). Prefer updating callers over keeping a permanent alias.

## Rename-only variables

If a variable is only assigned from another name and then used in its place, **drop the alias** and use the original.

```js
// ❌ BAD
const target = letter;
if (letters[i] !== target) continue;

// ✅ GOOD
if (letters[i] !== letter) continue;
```

Do not invent a new name “for clarity” when the existing parameter or binding already is that value.
